# Orb architecture

The Limbo orb is one WebGL point cloud in `LimboScene.tsx` (a `Points` object, about 5,600 particles). Each particle has a `home` on a procedural sphere. They are one group, so moving the group moves every particle. This is the same idea as the Dala brain (particle cloud whose homes came from a shape), not an SVG and not a solid mesh.

Current behavior — pointer follow, singularity on a world button, heartbeat, shed, stray pull — is specified in `site-architecture.mdc`. Keep it. The loop that implements it runs on the CPU inside `useFrame`. `SHELL` and `LOST` are per-particle state: a stray stores a velocity and an escape timer, and landing rewrites `home` to the arrival point.

## Do this next

Scroll, a turn, and a flight move the **group**, not the particles one by one. Write the target into a ref and apply it in the existing `useFrame`, the way the pointer already moves the group. Driving `position` or `rotation` from React state on scroll re-renders the scene every frame and will stutter.

An explosion or a sound wave is an offset away from `home`, then the spring back. The shed stray is the small version of this. Add that offset in the current loop.

A morph (sphere to speaker, sphere to current orb, brain to bulb to planet) is a second home list of the **same count**. Ease each `home` toward the new list. The shell already chases `home`, so the shape change is smooth. On a world flight the sphere opens and yaws first, and the homes ease after it has arrived. No hard cut. The software flight does not morph yet. The sphere travels and stays a sphere.

## World entries

The music and software buttons do not open `/music` or `/software`. Those pages stay in the repo.

Enter sound plays on the Limbo page, from the button or from a horizontal scroll toward the left. Scrolling back toward the right reverses that flight: the chrome returns and the speaker eases into the orb again. That horizontal wheel is cancelled so the browser does not treat it as back/forward and open `/music`. Vertical scrolling stays on the studio zoom in `ArrivalPage` and does not open a world. The limbo chrome slides off to the right. The background stays. The singularity leaves the cursor and settles in the top-left, and it does not follow the pointer after that. On the way, the collapsed ring opens back into a sphere. That sphere yaws as a rigid orb while it travels to the top-left, and only then do the homes ease into a woofer seen from above, cone opening toward the bottom-right. The spin angle is not scaled down with the morph. Thumps are a few seconds apart. Each thump sends a ring of particles out of the cone; they keep drifting off the screen and do not fade out or return. Loose particles take a soft push as a crest passes and can leave with it. The right side stays clear for the music UI. A horizontal scroll toward the right is the software direction. Enter code, from the button or that scroll, plays on the Limbo page and does not open `/software`. The stage is music, software, or idle. Music and software do not run at the same time. From software, a scroll toward the left returns to Limbo and does not pass through the speaker. The rest of that swipe stays on Limbo. A later scroll opens the other world. The same is true leaving the speaker.

The software flight keeps the sphere. The collapsed ring opens back into a sphere. That sphere yaws as a rigid orb while it travels to the top-right, the mirror of the speaker's top-left, and it does not follow the pointer after that. The limbo chrome slides off to the left. The background stays. Homes do not leave the sphere. Scrolling back toward the left reverses the flight: the chrome returns and the sphere eases back to the cursor.

## What not to refactor

A `.gltf` mesh only supplies a new list of homes. It does not explode, scroll, or morph. Keep the procedural sphere until a shape actually needs authored points.

A vertex shader is a later upgrade for a denser look (instanced triangles, cursor glow, curl noise), and only after a measured frame drop. It is not required for scroll or morph. Stateless effects — swell, cursor push, explosion offset — are formulas of home and time and can move to the GPU. The stray lifecycle cannot: it remembers velocity, a timer, and a rewritten home. That needs a simulation buffer, not a one-pass shader. Leave it in JavaScript.

Reduced motion freezes the spin, the follow, the shed, the heartbeat, the collapse, and any flight or morph.
